Let me also explain the scope of the app so you know where we're going and the vision for this app. It was birthed as a way to replace book logging or existing limited logging infrastructures (desktop-only and mobile ones (they usually have poor UI, the mobile ones)). It was my uncle who brought it to my realization. He said for supermarkets who have multiple employees who handle checkout, most of them (or at least, in Nigeria anyway) still use desktop-only infrastructure. He wanted mobility. His employees should be able to check people out with their phones, and he would be able to view who did what and when on the app on his phone regardless of where he is. That was the vision 'Quik Shoping' was built on. But, then came a new vision: the elimination of queues. In Nigeria where I am and other countries, people have to wait in queues to pay for whatever they buy. This leads to supermarkets losing customers out of frustration and tiredness. How phenomenal would it be if people could walk in a store, pick up a product, scan the side of the product (or a wall where a QR code is on), be redirected to the web app (or the android app if installed), pay, show app-generated receipt to security personnel, and walk out?! That's the new vision. The old and new visions are not different. In fact, they complement each other. What do I mean? A store that wants people to purchase products in literally 2 minutes without the friction of waiting in queues would need to use Quik Shopping as their Point of Sale. Not that it couldn't work otherwise, but as a marketing scheme. And, people already using Quik Shopping as the Point of Sale (POS) can 'upgrade' to vision 2, which solves the elimination of queues for no additional cost. The vision 2 would also allow people to check for stores around them, compare prices, and know how much to spend and where to spend all in the comfort of their house! What a vision! The pricing for all versions (or visions) of Quik Shopping is the same. A fixed 1.5% of all transactions and capped at 500 naira. This is for Nigerians but will evolve globally. 1% goes to payment providers (for accurate transfer  information) and remaining 0.5% goes to me. Payment provider's fee is capped at 300 naira, so remaining 200 goes to me. Payment provider will be Paystack or Monnify (chosen by me) to replace hardcoded transfer instance, preventing possible employee theft. The key thing here is digital temporary accounts. When someone wants to checkout with transfer, the app generates a one-time digital account for that specific transaction. For this, I believe their pricing is 1% capped at 300 naira per transaction. What do you think of this pricing model?

Also, vision 1 will be executed as verion 1 of Quik Shopping. We will roll this out when it's done, and immediately after, start working on vision (version) 2. 

